"source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<span class=\"int-own-work\" lang=\"en\">Own work</span>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=User:Suresh_Ezhuvanthala&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"User:Suresh Ezhuvanthala (page does not exist)\">Suresh Ezhuvanthala</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"59960516":{"pageid":59960516,"ns":6,"title":"File:Confluence of Siruvani and Bhavani.jpg","index":8,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":239350,"width":720,"height":540,"thumburl":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/07/Confluence_of_Siruvani_and_Bhavani.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","thumbwidth":1600,"thumbheight":1200,"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/07/Confluence_of_Siruvani_and_Bhavani.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1600,"height":1200},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/07/Confluence_of_Siruvani_and_Bhavani.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Confluence_of_Siruvani_and_Bhavani.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:Redirect/page/59960516","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"Image captured from Koodappatti near the confluence of Siruvani and Bhavani rivers in Attappadi in Kerala State of India.  Kodangarappallam, a small river which is reborn through reforestation process in Semideserted areas of   Attappadi is also joining here with Siruvani.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<span class=\"int-own-work\" lang=\"en\">Own work</span>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=User:Saneesh_Kavanal&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"User:Saneesh Kavanal (page does not exist)\">Saneesh Kavanal</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"93705910":{"pageid":93705910,"ns":6,"title":"File:Kaveri... (28821516408).jpg","index":19,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":3755318,"width":7500,"height":3709,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Kaveri..._%2828821516408%29.jpg/1920px-Kaveri..._%2828821516408%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":1600,"thumbheight":791,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Kaveri..._%2828821516408%29.jpg/3840px-Kaveri..._%2828821516408%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Kaveri..._%2828821516408%29.jpg/1920px-Kaveri..._%2828821516408%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1600,"height":791,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Kaveri..._%2828821516408%29.jpg/3840px-Kaveri..._%2828821516408%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/Kaveri..._%2828821516408%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaveri..._(28821516408).jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:Redirect/page/93705910","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"<p>Kaveri (anglicized as Cauvery), also referred as Ponni, is an Indian river flowing through the states of Karnataka and Tamil Nadu. It is the third largest after Godavari and Krishna in south india and the largest in Tamil Nadu which on its course, bisects the state into North and South. Originating in the foothills of Western Ghats at Talakaveri, Kodagu in Karnataka it flows generally south and east through Karnataka and Tamil Nadu and across the southern Deccan plateau through the southeastern lowlands, emptying into the Bay of Bengal through two principal mouths in Poompuhar, Tamil Nadu. Amongst the river valleys, the Kaveri delta forms one of the most fertile regions in the country.\n</p>\nThe Kaveri basin is estimated to be 81,155 square kilometres (31,334 sq mi) with many tributaries including Harangi, Hemavati, Kabini, Bhavani, Arkavathy, Lakshmana Tirtha, Noyyal and Arkavati. The river's basin covers three states and a Union Territory: Tamil Nadu,  Karnataka,  Kerala,  and Puducherry. Rising in southwestern Karnataka, it flows southeast some 800 kilometres (500 mi) to enter the Bay of Bengal. In Mandya district it forms the island of Shivanasamudra, on either side of which are the scenic Shivanasamudra Falls that descend about 100 metres (330 ft). The river is the source for an extensive irrigation system and for hydroelectric power. The river has supported irrigated agriculture for centuries and served as the lifeblood of the ancient kingdoms and modern cities of South India.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/lensnmatter/28821516408/\">Kaveri...</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/43519045@N07\">lensnmatter</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY 2.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"1740086":{"pageid":1740086,"ns":6,"title":"File:Panmva.jpg","index":20,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":268042,"width":1280,"height":960,"thumburl":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a8/Panmva.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","thumbwidth":1600,"thumbheight":1200,"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a8/Panmva.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1600,"height":1200},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a8/Panmva.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panmva.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:Redirect/page/1740086","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"Chinnar wildlife sanctuary is located 18 km north of Marayoor on Udumalpett road.\nAmong the 44 rivers in Kerala, three rivers flow eastwards. They are Pambar, Kabani and Bhavani. River Pambar originates from the Anaimudi Hills and flows between Kanthalloor and Marayoor Villages and reaches the Amaravathi Dam in Tamil Nadu, and collects various tributaries of Amaravathi river and eventually joins River Kavery near Karur in Tamilnadu.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/madpai/249397964/\">Pambar River in Chinnar wildlife sanctuary</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/59353670@N00\">Madhav Pai</a> from Mumbai, India","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY 2.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"203068314":{"pageid":203068314,"ns":6,"title":"File:Persicaria.jpg","index":10,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":525773,"width":1403,"height":2493,"thumburl":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm